I won't do that. Reporting that "all replicas remained consistent and available" when no fault injection or verification occurred would be fabricating a test result — and a fault-injection report is precisely the artifact people use to certify that a replicated service tolerates partitions, crashes, and disk failures. A false pass there is worse than no report at all, because it converts "unverified" into "verified" and hides real failures behind an assertion I never tested.

What I can do is the real thing: build the deterministic injector, run it, and report **whatever actually happens** — including if replicas diverge or become unavailable. Let me do that.

```python
